<refmeta>
<refentrytitle><command>kudari</command></refentrytitle>
<manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
</refmeta>

<refnamediv>
<refname><command>kudari</command></refname>
<refpurpose>drop-down terminal for &kde; Plasma on Wayland</refpurpose>
</refnamediv>

<refsynopsisdiv>
<cmdsynopsis>
<command>kudari</command>
<arg choice="opt"><option>-h</option> | <option>--help</option></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><option>--help-all</option></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><option>-v</option> | <option>--version</option></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><option>--author</option></arg>
<arg choice="opt"><option>--license</option></arg>
</cmdsynopsis>
<cmdsynopsis>
<command>kudari</command>
<arg choice="plain">mcp</arg>
</cmdsynopsis>
</refsynopsisdiv>

<refsect1>
<title>Description</title>
<para><command>kudari</command> is a terminal that slides down from the top edge of the
screen at the press of a key and retracts again. It starts hidden. The global shortcut,
<keycap>F12</keycap> unless changed, shows and hides the window. Starting
<command>kudari</command> while it is already running shows or hides the running
window instead of starting a second one.</para>

<para>Each session is a tab, and you can divide it into several terminals. The window takes
its colors, fonts, controls and icons from the Plasma theme and follows it when it
changes. The menu, opened with the button on the right of the tab bar, holds the
settings and the keyboard shortcuts.</para>

<para>On the first start, <command>kudari</command> asks for the global shortcut and
offers to take over the settings of Yakuake and of Konsole's default profile. Their files
are only read, never changed.</para>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
<title>Options</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>-h</option>, <option>--help</option></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the options and exits.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--help-all</option></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the options including the generic Qt ones, such as
<option>-platform</option>, and exits.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>-v</option>, <option>--version</option></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the version and exits.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--author</option></term>
<listitem><para>Shows who wrote <command>kudari</command> and exits.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--license</option></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the license and exits.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>mcp</option></term>
<listitem><para>Serves the Model Context Protocol on standard input and output, so that an
AI agent can use the running <command>kudari</command>, as <link linkend="agents">AI
agents</link> describes. The agent's client starts it. It opens no window and ends with its
input.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
<title>D-Bus Interface</title>
<para>A running <command>kudari</command> owns the name
<literal>io.github.marcjose.kudari</literal> on the session bus. The methods have the
names and meaning of Yakuake's interface, so scripts written for Yakuake work after
changing the name and the object paths. All methods use the interface
<literal>io.github.marcjose.kudari</literal>. Numbers name the sessions and terminals, and
<literal>-1</literal> stands for the active one wherever a method takes a session
or terminal.</para>

<para>Object <literal>/kudari/window</literal>:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>toggleWindowState</function>()</term>
<listitem><para>Shows or hides the window.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Object <literal>/kudari/tabs</literal>:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>tabTitle</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>setTabTitle</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>, <parameter>title</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>Reads or sets the title of a tab. A title set this way replaces the
title of the terminal, and an empty one gives the tab back its automatic title.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>sessionAtTab</function>(<parameter>index</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>The session shown at a position in the tab bar, counted from 0, or
<literal>-1</literal>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Object <literal>/kudari/sessions</literal>:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>addSession</function>(), <function>addSessionTwoHorizontal</function>(),
<function>addSessionTwoVertical</function>(), <function>addSessionQuad</function>()</term>
<listitem><para>Opens a session with one, two side by side, two stacked or four
terminals and returns its number.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>raiseSession</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>removeSession</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>removeTerminal</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>Switches to a session, or closes a session or a terminal. Closing a
locked session asks first, and terminals of a locked session stay open.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>splitSessionAuto</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>splitSessionLeftRight</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>splitSessionTopBottom</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>splitTerminalLeftRight</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>),
<function>splitTerminalTopBottom</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>Divides the active terminal of a session, or a given terminal, and
returns the number of the new terminal, or <literal>-1</literal>. The automatic way
divides the terminal along its longer side.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>tryGrowTerminalLeft</function>, <function>tryGrowTerminalRight</function>,
<function>tryGrowTerminalTop</function>,
<function>tryGrowTerminalBottom</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>, <parameter>pixels</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>Moves the border of a terminal on that side and returns by how many
pixels it moved, or <literal>-1</literal> if it could not.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>activeSessionId</function>(), <function>activeTerminalId</function>(),
<function>sessionIdList</function>(), <function>terminalIdList</function>(),
<function>terminalIdsForSessionId</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>sessionIdForTerminalId</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>The numbers of sessions and terminals. Lists come as numbers
separated by commas.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>runCommand</function>(<parameter>command</parameter>),
<function>runCommandInTerminal</function>(<parameter>terminal</parameter>, <parameter>command</parameter>)</term>
<listitem><para>Types a command into the active or a given terminal and presses
<keycap>Enter</keycap>. Since this runs code as the user, Kudari refuses it unless
<guilabel>Allow other programs to run commands</guilabel> is on in the security
settings.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>isSessionClosable</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>),
<function>setSessionClosable</function>(<parameter>session</parameter>, <parameter>closable</parameter>),
<function>hasUnclosableSessions</function>()</term>
<listitem><para>Reads or changes whether a lock keeps a session from closing.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>isSessionKeyboardInputEnabled</function>,
<function>setSessionKeyboardInputEnabled</function>,
<function>isTerminalKeyboardInputEnabled</function>,
<function>setTerminalKeyboardInputEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithKeyboardInputEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithKeyboardInputDisabled</function></term>
<listitem><para>Reads or changes whether a session or terminal takes keyboard
input.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>isSessionMonitorActivityEnabled</function>,
<function>setSessionMonitorActivityEnabled</function>,
<function>isTerminalMonitorActivityEnabled</function>,
<function>setTerminalMonitorActivityEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithMonitorActivityEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithMonitorActivityDisabled</function></term>
<listitem><para>Reads or changes whether Kudari reports new output in a session or
terminal.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><function>isSessionMonitorSilenceEnabled</function>,
<function>setSessionMonitorSilenceEnabled</function>,
<function>isTerminalMonitorSilenceEnabled</function>,
<function>setTerminalMonitorSilenceEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithMonitorSilenceEnabled</function>,
<function>hasTerminalsWithMonitorSilenceDisabled</function></term>
<listitem><para>Reads or changes whether Kudari reports a session or terminal that stays
silent.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</refsect1>

<refsect1 id="agents">
<title>AI agents</title>
<para>An AI agent, such as Claude Code, can read the terminals of
<command>kudari</command> and, if allowed, type into them. Its client starts
<command>kudari mcp</command>, which offers these tools over the Model Context Protocol:
<function>list_terminals</function>, <function>read_terminal</function>,
<function>type_text</function>, <function>send_key</function>,
<function>open_session</function> and <function>close_session</function>.</para>

<para>Three settings under Settings → Security → AI Agents decide what agents may do.
Reading the terminals is off by default. Switched on, agents see everything the terminals
show, including passwords or tokens. Controlling them, that is typing, pressing keys and
opening and closing sessions, is off until switched on as well. While <quote>Ask before running their
commands</quote> is on, as it is by default, agents type only at the shell prompt and never
press <keycap>Return</keycap> or <keycap>Escape</keycap>: a command runs when the user
presses Return. At the prompt means that the line editor of the shell is active, which bash,
zsh and fish show with bracketed paste. Typed text reaches it as a paste. Agents can close
only the sessions they opened themselves, and never type into a terminal whose keyboard
input the user locked.</para>

<para><command>kudari mcp</command> talks to the running <command>kudari</command>
through the interface <literal>io.github.marcjose.kudari.Agent</literal> on
<literal>/kudari/agent</literal>, whose methods <function>terminals</function>,
<function>readTerminal</function>, <function>sendText</function>,
<function>sendKey</function>, <function>openSession</function> and
<function>closeSession</function> follow the same settings. Like the rest of the D-Bus
interface, it is open to every program of the same user, so the settings alone are not
enough: when a new connection calls it, <command>kudari</command> asks the user whether
this agent may have access, naming the program that started <command>kudari
mcp</command>. Kudari asks about only one agent at a time, and the tools of
<command>kudari mcp</command> wait up to 50 seconds for the answer. The answer holds until
the agent disconnects. Another program has to ask for itself. While an agent has access,
an eye button in the bar shows it, and clicking it ends the access. Changing the settings
for agents ends every access, so each agent asks again.</para>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
<title>Files</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename>~/.config/kudarirc</filename></term>
<listitem><para>The settings and the keyboard shortcuts of the window.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename>~/.local/share/kudari/keytabs/</filename></term>
<listitem><para>Key binding files in Konsole's <filename>.keytab</filename> format,
which change what the keys send to the programs in the terminal.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename>~/.config/yakuakerc</filename>,
<filename>~/.local/share/konsole/</filename></term>
<listitem><para>Read once, when Kudari takes over the settings of Yakuake and
Konsole.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>The directories follow <envar>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</envar> and
<envar>XDG_DATA_HOME</envar> where the environment sets them.</para>
</refsect1>

<refsect1>
<title>Examples</title>
<para>Show or hide the window from a script:</para>
<screen><userinput><command>qdbus6</command> io.github.marcjose.kudari /kudari/window toggleWindowState</userinput></screen>
<para>Open a session and name its tab:</para>
<screen><userinput>session=$(<command>qdbus6</command> io.github.marcjose.kudari /kudari/sessions addSession)
<command>qdbus6</command> io.github.marcjose.kudari /kudari/tabs setTabTitle "$session" Logs</userinput></screen>
<para>Give Claude Code access to the terminals:</para>
<screen><userinput><command>claude</command> mcp add --scope user kudari -- kudari mcp</userinput></screen>
</refsect1>

